(FRONTIER MODEL SLM BUYER PROFILE MEMO FOR ASHWIN) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(RECOMMENDATION) Tj
T*
(The Frontier Model SLM should start with closed-loop talent transformation and placement) Tj
T*
(intermediaries.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The reason is simple: the first market should be chosen for the quality of the proof) Tj
T*
(environment, not the size of the eventual market. Closed-loop intermediaries offer the best) Tj
T*
(combination of planning pain, operational control, outcomes visibility, and repeatability.) Tj
T*
(They are the strongest environment for proving that the model improves strategic workforce) Tj
T*
(planning in a way that is commercially legible and capable of making the model smarter over) Tj
T*
(time.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The recommended sequence is:) Tj
T*
(1. Closed-loop talent transformation and placement intermediaries) Tj
T*
(2. Employer-connected mission and workforce intermediaries) Tj
T*
(3. Community college apprenticeship and pathway programs) Tj
T*
(4. Large employers with strategic workforce planning pain) Tj
T*
(5. Higher-ed institutions with co-op and externship outcome closure) Tj
T*
(6. State workforce agencies and systems) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(WHY THIS ORDER WINS) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Closed-loop intermediaries are the best proof market) Tj
T*
(This lane sits closest to a usable loop across talent assessment, pathway design, employer) Tj
T*
(demand interpretation, deployment, and observable outcomes. That makes it the strongest) Tj
T*
(place to validate the model and improve it quickly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(It is also a real category, not a single-account thesis. Revature is the clearest target,) Tj
T*
(but SkillStorm, Dev10, Wiley Edge, FDM Group, Multiverse, and others show that the lane is) Tj
T*
(deep enough to support a repeatable GTM motion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Employer-connected workforce intermediaries are the best bridge market) Tj
T*
(This lane is not as strong as the first one for pure proof, but it is the strongest second) Tj
T*
(move. It broadens the story from a commercial workforce intermediary thesis into a larger) Tj
T*
(workforce-system thesis. It adds employer legitimacy, mobility logic, and pathway relevance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Year Up / YUPRO and Per Scholas are the clearest examples. This is the lane that helps early) Tj
T*
(proof become legible to a wider set of institutional buyers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Community college pathways are the best higher-ed-adjacent expansion lane) Tj
T*
(This lane preserves occupational specificity, employer linkage, and category repeatability) Tj
T*
(better than generic higher education. It is the most practical way to begin expanding into) Tj
T*
(institution-based pathway systems after proof and bridge-market entry.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. Large employers are the strongest scale market, but not the best starting market) Tj
T*
(Large employers likely offer the largest direct TAM, highest ACVs, and richest KPI-level) Tj
T*
(outcomes data in theory. But they also come with harder data access, slower proof cycles,) Tj
T*
(heavier integration burden, and a higher risk that early wins become bespoke rather than) Tj
T*
(reusable.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This makes them an important scale market, but not the cleanest first proof market.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. Higher-ed with co-op / externship closure is high-quality but narrower) Tj
T*
(Institutions like Northeastern and Drexel are compelling because they partially solve higher) Tj
T*
(ed's usual outcome-closure problem. But the category appears more dependent on outliers and) Tj
T*
(less broadly repeatable than community college pathways.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6. State workforce systems are a later infrastructure play) Tj
T*
(State systems matter strategically, but they are slower, heavier, and more procurement-bound) Tj
T*
(than the earlier lanes. They should be approached after the product and evidence base are) Tj
T*
(more mature.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(COMPRESSED METHODOLOGY) Tj
T*
(The sequencing is based on a structured evaluation of each buyer profile against the same) Tj
T*
(core questions:) Tj
T*
(- How painful is the planning problem?) Tj
ET
